\chapter{Scaffolding a project or source library}
\label{ch:61}
\index{Scaffolding a project or source library}

\OnxCodeTerm{on1xc init --bin}, \OnxCodeTerm{--proj}, and \OnxCodeTerm{--lib} create executable, multi-target, and source-library scaffolds. Use the project root as the working directory for \OnxCodeTerm{on1xc exec build} or the selected profile. The scaffold owns a root manifest and participating directory manifests. It gives the loader a place to define inputs and the driver a place to define target policy.

\section{Grow a scaffold incrementally}
Build the generated starting target, edit one function to make its result observable, and rebuild. Add a library directory that defines a helper, declare the file in its local manifest, and import it using its package identity. Try the build from a clean checkout without stale outputs or private search-path settings. A new source file that is present but undeclared can fail discovery even when the function checks in single-file mode. Separate project target resolution from the actual source type error before editing the helper.

\section{Choose a product by its consumer}
Suppose a greeting has two consumers: a command that prints it and a second
project that imports it. Start with the command in a fresh directory:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xc init --bin hello
cd hello
on1xc exec build
./target/dev/hello
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
The last line runs the published target, whereas the preceding line prints
the path of its output directory. The starting command prints
\OnxCodeTerm{Hello from On1x!}. Change the text in
\OnxCodeTerm{src/main.on1x} and run only the last line again. The old
greeting is the expected result: the launcher here is a built executable,
not an interpreter reading the source at startup. Rebuild before testing
the changed greeting.

For the other consumer, initialize a sibling directory with
\OnxCodeTerm{on1xc init --lib greetings}. Inspect its
\OnxCodeTerm{src/\_\_init\_\_.on1x}. A small client of the generated
library can import that file and call \OnxCodeTerm{greeting()}; a library
build publishes checked source under the profile's library directory. Looking
for an executable named after the library would test the wrong product. Use
a complex project instead when the intended deliverables are several named
commands, each with its own entry file.

What if the destination already contains a hand-written entry? The driver
test plants the prospective source file before initialization. It checks
both that its contents survive and that no root manifest appears on failure.
An unrelated existing file, on the other hand, can coexist with a new
scaffold. Initialization therefore depends on collisions at planned paths,
not simply on whether the destination directory is empty.

